## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of multi-environment deployments without code duplication using provider aliases and module iteration.

## Key Concepts
- Provider aliases
- Module instantiation with for_each
- Per-environment variable files and backends
- Terraform plan/apply targeting to avoid cross-env changes

## Code Example
```terraform
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "prod"
  region = var.prod_region
}
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "dev"
  region = var.dev_region
}
variable "prod_region" { default = "us-east-1" }
variable "dev_region"  { default = "us-west-2" }
variable "environments" {
  type = map(string)
  default = { prod = "prod", dev = "dev" }
}
module "network" {
  for_each = var.environments
  source   = "./modules/network"
  providers = { aws = aws[each.value] }
  # inputs per env
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you add a new environment without touching existing state?
- What are the trade-offs of using separate backends versus workspaces for this pattern?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical gating of Terraform plans using a dedicated QA backend, plan export, and automated validation to prevent prod touches.

## Key Concepts
- Backend aliasing and per-env state isolation
- terraform plan output in JSON
- Scripting plan validation with jq/Node.js
- CI gating and manual approvals for apply

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js snippet to validate plan.json plan boundaries
const fs=require('fs');
const plan=JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('plan.json','utf8'));
const resources=plan?.planned_values?.root_module?.resources||[];
const ok=resources.every(r=>r.address.startsWith('modules/qa/')||r.address.includes('environment=qa'));
process.exit(ok?0:1);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to multi-module repos?
- How do you handle drift checks beyond plan validation?
